<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daimyo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The daimyo were the great feudal lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> who ruled domains and commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> retainers. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> about 260 daimyo governed their domains semi-autonomously, subject to shogunal regulation and the system of alternate attendance in Edo. The daimyo domains were abolished in 1871 after the Meiji Restoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daimyo.jpg" alt="Daimyo" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Japan_Genki1-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title is written with dai, meaning large, and myō, which stands for myōden, private land. The first to hold it were the shugo daimyō, who arose from among the shugo during the Muromachi period, approximately 1336 to 1573. They held military and police powers and economic power within a province, and the largest ruled several provinces. The Ashikaga shogunate required them to live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, so they appointed relatives or retainers, called shugodai, to represent them in their home provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a>, in which shugo-daimyō fought each other, provincial uprisings took place and the deputies living in the provinces strengthened their position. At the end of the fifteenth century those shugo-daimyō who had controlled their deputies stayed in power, and the others were replaced by a new class, the sengoku-daimyō, who rose from the ranks of the shugodai and the jizamurai. These were de facto independent lords whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> and peasants were loyal to them rather than to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, in other words warlords.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600 Shōgun Tokugawa Ieyasu reorganised the daimyo and their territories into han, assessed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> production, and only those heading a han of 10,000 koku or more counted as daimyo. He divided them by their relation to the Tokugawa: the shinpan, who were related to it, the fudai, who had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> or allies, and the tozama, who had not allied with it before Sekigahara. Only fudai could hold posts in the shogunate, whereas tozama in general could not.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Daimyo often hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> to guard their land and paid them in land or food, since relatively few could afford to pay in money. Under the Tokugawa they had to keep residences in Edo as well as in their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> and to spend alternate years in each, a practice called sankin-kōtai. The Tokugawa at first regarded the tozama as potentially rebellious, but such control policies led to peaceful relations for most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>.</p><p>In 1869, the year after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, the daimyo and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuge">kuge</a> formed a new aristocracy, the kazoku. The han were abolished in 1871 and replaced by prefectures, and the daimyo and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> were pensioned off. Many daimyo were at first appointed governors but were soon called to Tokyo, which cut off any independent base for rebellion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daimyo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The institutions through which the shogunate governed were worked out over the following decades: the classification of <b>daimyo</b> as Tokugawa kin (shinpan), hereditary vassals (fudai) or 'outside' lords (tozama); the Buke Shohatto regulations of 1615; and the sankin-kotai system of alternate attendance, formalised by the third shogun Iemitsu…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As in almost all Japanese castles from the Azuchi-Momoyama period onward, the keep was used as a storehouse in peacetime and as a fortified tower in war, while the <b>daimyo</b>'s offices and residences stood in single-storey buildings nearby.</blockquote><a href="/work/himeji-castle">Himeji Castle · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Above the samurai stood the <b>daimyo</b>, the shogun who ruled from Edo, and, highest in honour but without power, the emperor and his court in Kyoto.</blockquote><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Social Hierarchy of Tokugawa Japan</a></li></ul></section>
